outputs.L1:
I hear America singing—the varied carols I hear:  
The mechanics, each singing his own, as it should be, blithe and strong;  
The carpenter singing as he measures his plank or beam;  
The mason singing as he prepares for work, or as he leaves off work;  
The boatman singing what belongs to him in his boat; the deckhand singing on the steamboat deck;  
The shoemaker singing as he sits at his bench; the hatter singing as he stands;  
The wood-cutter’s song; the ploughboy’s, on his way in the morning, at the noon intermission, or at sundown;  
The delightful singing of the mother, of the young wife at work, or of the girl sewing or washing;  
Each singing what belongs to him or her, and to none else;  
The day singing what belongs to the day—  
At night, the gathering of young fellows, robust and friendly,  
Singing with open mouths their strong, melodious songs.

outputs.L2:
I perceive America in song, and I hear many different tunes:  
the mechanics, each giving voice to his own lively and sturdy music;  
the carpenter singing while he sizes up his board or timber;  
the mason singing as he prepares to begin his labor, or as he finishes it;  
the boatman singing the song proper to his place in the boat, and the deckhand singing on the deck of the steamboat;  
the shoemaker singing from his bench, and the hatter singing while standing at his work;  
the woodcutter’s tune, and the ploughboy’s song as he goes along in the morning, during the midday pause, or at sunset;  
the sweet song of the mother, of the young wife busy with her tasks, or of the girl sewing or washing;  
each person singing the music that is uniquely his or hers, and no one else’s;  
daytime holding the songs that belong to the day, and, at night, a gathering of young men, strong and companionable,  
singing aloud their powerful, tuneful songs.
